{
  // Fail CI on high or critical advisories. Moderates/lows are still reported but non-blocking,
  // matching the prior `npm audit --audit-level=high` policy.
  "high": true,

  // Pass when the audit itself cannot be completed — e.g. npm's advisory endpoint
  // (registry.npmjs.org/-/npm/v1/security/advisories/bulk) returns errors, which it
  // does intermittently and which audit-ci reports as "code undefined: Exiting...".
  // audit-ci already retries (retry-count default 5); when even that fails, the whole
  // merge queue stalls on an external outage that is NOT a vulnerability. This makes
  // audit-ci treat an unreachable endpoint as a pass while STILL failing on real
  // high/critical advisories whenever the endpoint is reachable. (Stalled the queue
  // on 2026-07-26.)
  "pass-enoaudit": true,

  // Advisories listed here are explicitly accepted. Each entry MUST have a tracking note
  // explaining why it's safe and when it can be removed.
  //
  // NOTE on the MODERATE entries below (uuid, decode-uri-component): because the gate
  // above is `"high": true`, audit-ci never evaluates moderates and will print them under
  // "Consider not allowlisting advisories" as if they were unused. They are not unused —
  // they are the written record of a decision, kept here because this file is the repo's
  // stated home for accepted advisories. Do not delete them on the strength of that line;
  // check the advisory's own severity first.
  "allowlist": [
    // GHSA-3jxr-9vmj-r5cp / GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg / GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895 — three
    // brace-expansion DoS advisories (all high), each published above the previous
    // one's patch floor (<5.0.7, <5.0.8, <5.0.9 respectively). The only vulnerable
    // copy left is bundled INSIDE aws-cdk-lib
    // (node_modules/aws-cdk-lib/node_modules/brace-expansion@5.0.6 via its vendored
    // minimatch@10), which an npm `overrides` floor cannot reach — overrides do not
    // apply to a published package's bundledDependencies. aws-cdk-lib 2.261.0 still
    // bundles the vulnerable version (verified 2026-08-05). The reachable, non-bundled
    // copies are patched: the 1.x line at brace-expansion@1.1.16 (below every
    // advisory's >=4.0.0 floor anyway), and the 5.x line forced to >=5.0.9 by the
    // `brace-expansion@5` override in package.json (raised 2026-08-05 when
    // GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895 flipped the gate). Exposure is synth/CDK-time only over
    // trusted inputs — not a runtime path. Remove each entry once aws-cdk-lib bundles
    // brace-expansion >=5.0.9 (then audit-ci's "unused allowlist entry" report flags it).
    "GHSA-3jxr-9vmj-r5cp",
    "GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg",
    "GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895",

    // GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p (<5.0.10) / GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7 (<5.0.11) — two more
    // brace-expansion stack-exhaustion DoS advisories (both high), landed 2026-09-30,
    // above the >=5.0.9 floor the three entries above were allowlisted against. Exactly
    // the recurrence that entry predicted, and the same split: the reachable non-bundled
    // copy is patched by raising the `brace-expansion@5` override to >=5.0.12 in
    // package.json (which also clears the moderate GHSA-q2hr-2g5m-vwhr, <5.0.12), while
    // the copy bundled inside aws-cdk-lib's vendored minimatch@10 cannot be reached by
    // an npm `overrides` floor at all — overrides do not apply to a published package's
    // bundledDependencies. Exposure is synth/CDK-time only over our own committed
    // infrastructure definitions, not a runtime path over untrusted input. Remove each
    // entry once aws-cdk-lib bundles brace-expansion >=5.0.12 (audit-ci's "Consider not
    // allowlisting advisories" report is what flags it as unused).
    "GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p",
    "GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7",

    // GHSA-m9gg-hp2v-232j — @grpc/grpc-js getAuthContext can return unauthorized
    // certificates as though they were authorized (high, vulnerable >=1.14.0 <1.14.5).
    // Landed 2026-09-30. One copy, hoisted to the root: apps/api >
    // @temporalio/client@1.24.0 > @grpc/grpc-js@1.14.4.
    //
    // NOT REACHABLE HERE: `getAuthContext` is a gRPC *server* API, read off a
    // ServerSurfaceCall to inspect a peer's mTLS certificate. This repo has no gRPC
    // server — `@temporalio/client` is the only @temporalio/* package in apps/api and it
    // dials Temporal Cloud as a client; there is no grpc.Server construction anywhere in
    // apps/ or packages/. The companion GHSA-f596-whhp-79r4 (low, same range) is
    // server-side for the same reason: it leaks handler error messages to clients.
    //
    // WHY AN ALLOWLIST AND NOT AN OVERRIDE, which is the part worth not re-deriving:
    // `"@grpc/grpc-js": ">=1.14.5"` in `overrides` is a NO-OP on this tree. npm applies
    // it — `npm ls` prints `@grpc/grpc-js@1.14.4 overridden` — and still resolves 1.14.4.
    // Neither documented remedy works: `npm update @grpc/grpc-js --package-lock-only`
    // followed by `npm install` leaves 1.14.4 in place, and `npm install
    // --package-lock-only` does not re-resolve it either. Deleting the single
    // `node_modules/@grpc/grpc-js` lock key PRUNES the package outright (`npm ls` →
    // `(empty)`), which is the exact failure mode GOTCHAS `npm overrides: two mechanics`
    // records for protobufjs and qs. All three were tried on 2026-09-30 and reverted.
    //
    // The real fix is upstream: @temporalio/client declares `@grpc/grpc-js: ^1.12.4`, so
    // a client bump that re-resolves the tree will pick up >=1.14.5 on its own. Remove
    // this entry then (audit-ci's "Consider not allowlisting advisories" report flags it).
    "GHSA-m9gg-hp2v-232j",

    // GHSA-w3rx-r6r6-pgpr / GHSA-5p2g-fcmc-qvqq — two image-size DoS advisories
    // (both high): infinite loops in the ICNS parser and in the JXL/HEIF parsers.
    //
    // A PATCH NOW EXISTS — 2.0.3, published after this entry was first written
    // (both advisories are `<=2.0.2`). It is still not usable here, for a
    // different reason than before: image-size 2.x is a BREAKING major that
    // metro cannot take. metro@0.83.7 declares `image-size: ^1.0.2` and calls it
    // at node_modules/metro/src/Assets.js:177 with a FILE PATH STRING
    // (`assetInfo.files[0]`); 2.x dropped string/path input from the main entry
    // (it moved to an async `fromFile` export) and throws
    // `TypeError: The "list" argument must be an instance of ... ArrayBufferView`
    // on a string — verified directly against 2.0.3 on 2026-09-29. Forcing the
    // override would break asset sizing for every image in the mobile bundle.
    // (The earlier rationale recorded here — "no patched version exists", checked
    // 2026-08-07 — is what changed; the conclusion did not.) npm's `fixAvailable`
    // still suggests expo@46.0.21, a major DOWNGRADE from the installed expo@55,
    // which is not a fix either.
    //
    // Single path, build-time only:
    //   apps/mobile > expo@55.0.29 > @expo/metro > metro@0.83.7 > image-size@1.2.1
    // That is Metro, the React Native bundler, sizing the app's own committed image
    // assets during a build. The inputs are repo-controlled, not user-supplied, and
    // no runtime request path reaches image-size — nothing in apps/api or the web
    // apps depends on it. A DoS on our own build inputs is not a live exposure.
    //
    // Upstream HAS already moved: metro@0.87.1 has no image-size dependency at
    // all (checked 2026-09-29). The ceiling holding us on metro 0.83.7 is
    // therefore Expo SDK 55 -> react-native 0.83 -> metro 0.83, not image-size.
    // Remove when apps/mobile lands on an Expo SDK whose metro has dropped or
    // upgraded image-size — audit-ci reports unused allowlist entries.
    "GHSA-w3rx-r6r6-pgpr",
    "GHSA-5p2g-fcmc-qvqq",

    // GHSA-ggr8-5vv4-36mx — deepmerge-ts stack exhaustion (high, CWE-674) when
    // merging recursive object graphs. Vulnerable range is `<8.0.0`; the fix is
    // the 8.x major.
    //
    // Allowlisted rather than overridden because @prisma/config depends on
    // deepmerge-ts by EXACT pin ("deepmerge-ts": "7.1.5", verified in
    // node_modules/@prisma/config/package.json at prisma 7.8.0). Forcing an
    // override across a major the vendor pinned exactly is not a fix — it swaps
    // a build-time DoS for an unsupported prisma CLI. There is no in-range patch
    // to point a floor at.
    //
    // Single path, build-time only, and dev-only at that:
    //   apps/api > (devDependency) prisma@7.8.0 > @prisma/config@7.8.0 > deepmerge-ts@7.1.5
    // That is the Prisma CLI merging our own committed prisma config during
    // `prisma generate` / `prisma migrate`. `prisma` is a devDependency — it is
    // not bundled into the API Lambda and no request path reaches it. The only
    // object graph it merges is repo-controlled, so "recursive input causes
    // stack exhaustion" has no untrusted source to come from.
    //
    // Remove once prisma ships a @prisma/config on deepmerge-ts >=8.0.0
    // (audit-ci reports unused allowlist entries once it does).
    "GHSA-ggr8-5vv4-36mx",

    // GHSA-w5hq-g745-h8pq — uuid missing a buffer bounds check (moderate, <11.1.1).
    //
    // Accepted on REACHABILITY, not on severity. The advisory is specific: it fires
    // only in `v3`, `v5` and `v6` and only when the optional `buf` output argument
    // is passed. Neither consumer does either. Both call sites were read directly
    // (2026-09-29) and both use `v4()` with no arguments:
    //   exceljs@4.4.0    lib/xlsx/xform/sheet/cf-ext/cf-rule-ext-xform.js:1
    //                    `const {v4: uuidv4} = require('uuid')`  -> uuid@8.3.2
    //   xcode@3.0.1      lib/pbxProject.js:90  `var id = uuid.v4()`  -> uuid@7.0.3
    //                    (via apps/mobile > expo > @expo/config-plugins)
    // The root copy is already patched at 11.1.1 (@temporalio/client); only these
    // two old transitive copies are in range.
    //
    // NOT overridden, deliberately. Forcing >=11.1.1 would drag exceljs across
    // THREE majors (8 -> 11) and xcode across FOUR (7 -> 11), on packages that
    // never asked for it, to fix a code path neither one calls. That is exactly
    // the forced-major churn CLAUDE.md's dependency rule warns against.
    //
    // Remove once exceljs and @expo/config-plugins ship uuid >=11.1.1.
    "GHSA-w5hq-g745-h8pq",

    // GHSA-vcc3-ghjq-m6fr — decode-uri-component DoS via exponential decoding of
    // malformed percent-encoded input (moderate, <=0.4.2; fix is 0.5.0).
    //
    // Allowlisted because THE FIX IS UNINSTALLABLE ON THE VULNERABLE PATHS.
    // decode-uri-component@0.5.0 is ESM-only (`"type": "module"`, a single
    // `export default function`), but both vulnerable copies sit under
    // query-string@7.1.3, which is CommonJS and consumes it as
    // `const decodeComponent = require('decode-uri-component')` (index.js:3).
    // Probed directly on 2026-09-29: under Node 24 that `require()` returns
    // `{__esModule, default}`, so the call site dies with
    // `decodeComponent is not a function`. An override here does not harden the
    // path, it breaks it — the same ESM-only trap the `nanoid` note in
    // package.json documents for nanoid 4+.
    //
    // Two paths, both client-side in the mobile app, both on query-string@7.1.3:
    //   apps/mobile > @react-navigation/drawer > .../core > query-string@7.1.3
    //   apps/mobile > expo-router@55.0.18       > query-string@7.1.3
    // The root copy is already patched: tenant-web resolves query-string@9.5.1,
    // which depends on decode-uri-component@0.5.0. The remaining exposure is a
    // hostile deep link hanging the app on the user's OWN device — there is no
    // server-side path, and nothing in apps/api or the web apps reaches it.
    //
    // Upstream is already half-fixed: @react-navigation/core@7.23.0 dropped
    // query-string entirely (checked 2026-09-29), so bumping react-navigation
    // clears the first path. expo-router still declares query-string ^7.1.3 as
    // of 57.0.24, and apps/mobile is pinned to the 55.x line by Expo SDK 55, so
    // the second path cannot be cleared from here yet.
    //
    // Remove once both parents are off query-string@7.
    "GHSA-vcc3-ghjq-m6fr",

    // GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f — decode-uri-component DoS via quadratic replacement of
    // percent-encoded runs (HIGH, <=0.5.0; fix is 0.5.1). Published 2026-10-29.
    //
    // Same two paths and the same reason as GHSA-vcc3 directly above:
    // decode-uri-component@0.5.1 is still ESM-only (`"type": "module"`), so it
    // can't be installed under the CommonJS query-string@7.1.3 in apps/mobile
    // (react-navigation core and expo-router). The tenant-web copy (under
    // query-string@9.5.1, `^0.5.0`) WAS bumped to 0.5.1 in the same change, so
    // the only exposure left is a hostile deep link hanging the app on the
    // user's own device. There is no server-side path.
    //
    // Remove together with GHSA-vcc3, once both parents are off query-string@7.
    "GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f",

    // GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm — braces stack-exhaustion denial of service through deeply nested
    // patterns (high, CWE-674). Published 2026-09-18 and surfaced in this repo's audit on
    // 2026-10-03; it ejected #772 from the merge queue and is the only advisory failing the gate.
    // Reproduced against an unmodified lockfile first, so it is main's and not any PR's.
    //
    // Allowlisted because NO PATCHED VERSION EXISTS — the same reason node-forge's
    // GHSA-86w9-cpqp-85rv was allowlisted on 2026-10-02 (removed 2026-11-02, once node-forge
    // 1.4.1 shipped). The vulnerable range is `<=3.0.3` and 3.0.3 IS the
    // latest published braces (`npm view braces version`, 2026-10-03); `first_patched_version` is
    // null. There is no floor for an `overrides` entry to point at. **Check that field before
    // planning an override** — one `gh api /advisories/<GHSA>` call settles whether a fix is even
    // possible.
    //
    // ONE copy, hoisted to the root at braces@3.0.3, with exactly one direct dependent in the
    // tree — `micromatch@^3.0.3`. micromatch in turn is reached only by Metro and Jest:
    //   apps/mobile > expo > @expo/metro-config > @expo/metro > metro-file-map > micromatch
    //   apps/mobile > react-native > babel-jest > @jest/transform > micromatch
    //   (plus jest-haste-map and jest-message-util, same Jest tree)
    // That is the React Native BUNDLER and the TEST RUNNER globbing file paths, at build and test
    // time. Same exposure shape as the image-size entry below, which is also Metro over our own
    // committed assets: the glob patterns and paths are repo-controlled, not user-supplied, and no
    // runtime request path in apps/api or the web apps reaches micromatch or braces at all. A
    // stack-exhaustion DoS needs a hostile pattern, and nothing hostile reaches a build glob.
    //
    // Remove once braces publishes a release the advisory does not cover and micromatch re-resolves
    // onto it (audit-ci's "Consider not allowlisting advisories" report flags this entry once it is
    // unused). Note micromatch pins `^3.0.3`, so a 3.0.4 would be picked up by a plain install
    // while a 4.x would need micromatch to move first.
    "GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm",

    // GHSA-mrhx-c7xc-m3w5 — zod can exhaust memory validating oversized constrained collections
    // (high, vulnerable <4.6.6). Landed 2026-10-23 and turned main red. The 4.x copies (apps/api,
    // @hono/zod-openapi, stryker) were moved to 4.6.7 in the lockfile; that is the reachable
    // runtime one. What is left is ONE copy on the 3.x line:
    //   apps/mobile > expo > @expo/cli > zod@3.25.76
    // That is the Expo developer CLI validating our own app config / CLI flags at dev and EAS
    // build time. It is not in the app bundle, and nothing user-supplied reaches it. There is no
    // fix to install: the advisory's only patched release is 4.6.6 (a major for @expo/cli, an API
    // break), and the latest @expo/cli (58.1.15 on 2026-10-25) still declares `zod: ^3.25.76`.
    //
    // NOTE this allowlist is by advisory id, so it would also hide a 4.x copy below 4.6.6
    // re-entering the tree. Check `npm ls zod` when touching zod. Remove once @expo/cli moves to
    // zod 4 (the "Consider not allowlisting advisories" report flags it once unused).
    "GHSA-mrhx-c7xc-m3w5",

    // GHSA-xcx6-jx8p-x3h5 — MSAL Node's authorization code flow may send parameters to an
    // attacker-controlled endpoint (high, vulnerable >=5.0.0-beta.0 <7.1.0). Landed 2026-10-23.
    // One copy: apps/mssql-executor > mssql > tedious > @azure/identity@4.13.1 >
    // @azure/msal-node@5.2.4.
    //
    // NOT REACHABLE HERE: the authorization code flow is an interactive OAuth sign-in (redirect
    // a user, exchange the returned code). tedious only ever uses @azure/identity's
    // non-interactive credentials, for its Azure AD connection modes. And mssql-executor opens
    // `new sql.ConnectionPool(connectionString)` with the tenant's SQL Server login connection
    // strings, so it never takes an Azure AD path at all.
    //
    // No override: 7.1.0 is a major past what @azure/identity declares (`^6.0.0` even in the
    // latest 4.14.0; tedious@20.3.8 pins identity `^4.13.1`), and forcing a major into a published
    // package's tree is the breakage GOTCHAS warns about. Remove once @azure/identity accepts
    // msal-node >=7.1.0 and the tree re-resolves.
    "GHSA-xcx6-jx8p-x3h5",

    // GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw — qs: `qs.stringify` with arrayFormat 'comma' throws an uncaught
    // TypeError on array elements carrying a non-callable own `toString` (moderate, vulnerable
    // >=6.7.0 <=6.16.0; fix is 6.16.1). Surfaced 2026-11-05.
    //
    // One copy, dev-only: packages/domain > @stryker-mutator/core > typed-rest-client (mutation
    // testing), which never runs in production. Same single path as the older
    // GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26, which the parent-scoped `typed-rest-client.qs` override in
    // package.json already carries a floor for.
    //
    // RAISING THAT FLOOR IS A REGRESSION, NOT A FIX — measured 2026-11-05. Moving it from
    // `>=6.15.2 <7` to `>=6.16.1 <7` makes the resolution go BACKWARDS, from 6.16.0 to 6.15.1
    // (typed-rest-client's exact pin, which the override then fails to lift). 6.15.1 is INSIDE
    // the older advisory's range (>=6.11.1 <=6.15.1), so raising the floor re-opens
    // GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26 while still not clearing this one. Confirmed two ways with identical
    // results — `npm update qs --package-lock-only` + `npm install`, and a full
    // `npm install --package-lock-only` — so it is not an artifact of which command was used.
    // Do not retry the floor; the exact pin has to move upstream.
    //
    // Remove once typed-rest-client relaxes or bumps its qs pin (the same condition as the
    // override entry in package.json), or once @stryker-mutator/core leaves packages/domain.
    "GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw",

    // GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249 — minimatch: potential denial of service via globparts amplification
    // (moderate, vulnerable >=7.0.0 <11.0.1; fix is 11.0.1). Surfaced 2026-11-05.
    //
    // Dependabot cannot fix this one: its security job has failed TWICE (runs 42029545563 and
    // 42073846265) with conflicting-dependencies — "the latest possible version that can be
    // installed is 10.2.6" — because no direct edge in the tree reaches 11.x.
    //
    // AND NEITHER CAN AN OVERRIDE — measured 2026-11-05. Raising the existing tree-wide floor
    // from `>=3.1.4` to `>=11.0.1` moves six copies to 11.0.4 but leaves three vulnerable, while
    // inflating the tree from 2 minimatch copies to 9:
    //   - node_modules/minimatch (root)                        stays 10.2.6
    //   - aws-cdk-lib/node_modules/minimatch                   stays 10.2.6
    //   - apps/mobile/.../glob/node_modules/minimatch          stays 9.0.9
    // The aws-cdk-lib copy is the bundled one the brace-expansion note in package.json already
    // carves out as unreachable by overrides; this entry is that same carve-out, now covering
    // the root and apps/mobile copies too.
    //
    // Remove once aws-cdk-lib and apps/mobile's glob pull minimatch >=11.0.1 and the root copy
    // re-resolves above the floor — at which point raise the package.json floor in the same
    // change rather than instead of it.
    "GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249",
  ],
}
